# The fusions of one part that eliminate nothing, beside the part they carry. One row per such
# fusion per quantity, with the part's figure and the composed figure side by side.
#
# Identity needs no arithmetic
#   Identity is the one case where composition needs no arithmetic at all, which is what makes it
#   the sharpest test in the file. A fusion of one part that eliminates nothing must reproduce that
#   part: there is nothing to sum, nothing to subtract, and no judgement to make. So any difference
#   is a defect with no defence, and it can be asked of every quantity rather than only the one the
#   sum rule composes.
#
# Five quantities, the sum rule's three and the slacks
#   `checks/fusion_sum_disagrees` reads composition/fused.sqlc, which sums demand, nameplate and
#   draw; `checks/window_lost_or_summed` covers the calendar. Nothing sums the three buffer slacks,
#   so this relation is where a one-part fusion is held to them.
#
# A suspension removes only the quantity it names
#   A fusion suspended for its draw alone still carries its demand and nameplate, so the
#   anti-join matches on the quantity, or on a NULL quantity, which suspends them all.
#
# A one-part fusion is not degenerate
#   `every-local-part` says so in its own words: "A one-part fusion is not degenerate: it says this
#   reviewer read that layer and carried it." Carrying is an assertion, so it is a thing that can
#   be got wrong.
#
# The factor still applies
#   A single part may be converted into the composed layer's unit, so identity is "the part,
#   converted" rather than "the part, copied".
#
# A factor filed as absent is excluded, not read as one
#   `coalesce(factor, 1)` is the fill this directory warns about wherever it appears: 1 is the
#   multiplicative identity, and filling an absence with an identity element is only sound where
#   the absence means measured and empty. `asrt:factor` is a `pm:StatedFactor`, so its absent arm
#   carries a typed reason, and `every-unsized-conversion/shift-capacity` files `unmeasured` on one
#   of its parts: a composer saying nobody measured the conversion. Read as 1 that becomes "the
#   units are the same", which is a claim they declined to make. Identity cannot be asserted across
#   a conversion nobody sized, so those parts leave this relation.
#
# An omitted factor is a different state, and it stays
#   `minOccurs="0"` and no factor means no conversion is needed, which is the sender asserting the
#   units already agree. That is a filing; `unmeasured` is a gap.
#
# Two quantity codomains meet here
#   A suspension names its quantity as `pm.summed_quantity`, the three a fusion sums; a candidate
#   carries `public.layer_quantity`, the five a layer states. Both are declared types and they are
#   not the same domain: they agree on `demand` and `nameplate` and on nothing else. So both sides
#   cast to text, which is not a workaround but the statement that the NAME is the whole of what
#   the two sets share. Drop either cast and Postgres refuses the comparison, which is the right
#   refusal: nothing implicitly converts one codomain into the other.
#
# The difference against a named candidate set
#   The candidate set is a file, composition/carriable.sqlc, and this is the difference taken
#   against it. Both operands are named, so algebra/carried.sqlc can count them. Fold the two
#   `NOT EXISTS` back into a `WHERE` over an inline candidate set and the law loses its A: the SQL
#   still runs, the rows are identical, and nothing on algebra/roster.sqlc can be written about it.
-- composition/carriable.sqlc less the fusions that eliminate or owe no sum.
SELECT c.*
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/carriable.sqlc)
) c
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1 FROM ( :compose(eliminations/filed.sqlc) ) e
        WHERE e.composition = c.filing AND e.composed_layer = c.layer)
  AND NOT EXISTS (
        SELECT 1 FROM ( :compose(composition/suspended_quantities.sqlc) ) s
        WHERE s.composition = c.filing AND s.composed_layer = c.layer
          AND s.quantity::text = c.quantity::text)
